For this analysis I chose Klondike Solitaire, the classic single-player card game played with a
standard 52-card deck, because it's simple surface hides rich structure that maps cleanly onto
several game design theories. After shuffling, the player deals seven tableau columns left to
right, with one card in the first column, two in the second, and so on up to seven in the seventh;
only the top card of each column is face up, and the remainder of the deck forms a stock that is
turned over into a waste pile as needed. The overarching goal is to build four foundations, one
per suit, from Ace up to King, while the tableau follows strict placement rules: cards stack in
descending order and must alternate color (for instance, a red 6 onto a black 7); 
when a facedown card becomes exposed, it flips face up; only a King (or a sequence starting with a King)
may be moved to an empty tableau column; and cards can be moved to foundations in ascending
order by suit. Depending on the variant, the player draws one or three cards from the stock at a
time, cycling through until a win is achieved (all foundations complete) or no legal moves
remain.
Framed this way, Solitaire clearly satisfies Maroney’s description of a game as “a form of play
with goals and structure”: the goal is unambiguous (complete the four foundations), and the
structure is exacting, from alternating-color stacks and suit-constrained foundations to the Kingonly 
rule for empty columns and the controlled pace of drawing from the stock. McGonigal’s
definition, “activities with goals, rules, feedback, and voluntary participation”, also fits cleanly,
and in a way that makes the design levers explicit: the goals and rules are as above; feedback is
immediate and legible in the changing layout (each flipped card, each foundation advance, each
blocked position communicates progress or stagnation); and voluntary participation is baked in,
since the player chooses to start, quit, or replay without external compulsion. Under
McGonigal’s lens, Solitaire is a poster child for how crisp rules and tight feedback loops create
engagement even in a solitary setting. Schell’s formulation, “a problem-solving activity
approached with a playful attitude”, casts Solitaire as a rolling constraint-satisfaction puzzle:
every decision trades short-term gains against future flexibility (for example, moving a card to a
foundation might seem like progress yet reduce tableau maneuverability; choosing which facedown card 
to expose first can shape the rest of the game). The “playful attitude” appears in the
low stakes and iterative experimentation that Solitaire invites: players often try risky move
orders, learn from dead-ends, and restart with the curiosity of “one more attempt,” which is the
ethos Schell emphasizes. The friction comes with Crawford’s definition, which requires “an
interactive, goal-oriented activity, with active agents to play against, in which players (including
active agents) can interfere with each other.” Solitaire is indeed interactive and goal-oriented, but
it lacks an active, adaptive opponent who can interfere in response to the player’s choices; the
shuffle introduces chance, not agency. No adversary makes countermoves; the only
“interference” is self-inflicted through suboptimal sequencing. Thus, under Crawford’s
taxonomy, Klondike lands outside “game” proper and nearer to “puzzle” or “contest against
nature,” a distinction that clarifies why facing a human in chess feels categorically different from
wrestling with a shuffled deck alone. Comparing the four definitions reveals a shared backbone,
goals and rules/structure are present in all but are weighted differently, while the decisive 
divergence is the requirement of opponent agency. Maroney and McGonigal comfortably include
Solitaire because they emphasize formal properties (goals, structure, rules, feedback,
voluntariness) that Solitaire exemplifies; Schell includes it because he privileges the cognitive
stance of playful problem-solving; Crawford excludes it to preserve a conceptual bright line
between puzzles and games, insisting that strategic interference among agents is central to
“game-ness.”
Personally, I find McGonigal’s definition the most convincing and practically useful, especially
for design and evaluation. It offers an operational checklist, goal clarity, rule precision, feedback
loops, and voluntariness, that predicts engagement and distinguishes free play from gameplay
without smuggling in a multiplayer bias; it accommodates single-player phenomena like Solitaire
or Wordle while still excluding passive media consumption that lacks rule-bound decisionmaking and 
in-system feedback. This lens also explains why small interface choices matter:
when Solitaire surfaces progress (e.g., a visible foundation count) or friction (e.g., a clear “no
more moves” signal), it heightens the sense of a closed, meaningful system. At the same time,
Crawford’s insistence on adversarial agency sharpened my intuition about the “feel” difference
between solo and competitive experiences: introduce an interfering opponent—say, a timed race
against an AI that locks columns or a head-to-head variant with drafting—and Solitaire would
cross Crawford’s boundary without changing its core mechanics. After doing this analysis,
Solitaire feels not less like a game but more precisely like a puzzle-forward game whose “gameness” 
depends on which theoretical lens you choose; the exercise clarified that “what counts as a
game” is not a single essence but a family resemblance organized around goals and rules, with
opponent agency functioning as the axis that moves an activity from meditative puzzle toward
strategic contest. In short, Klondike indisputably meets Maroney, McGonigal, and Schell; it fails
Crawford only on the active-opponent clause; and the comparative view deepens rather than
diminishes the category, showing how different emphases; formal structure, feedback, mindset,
or adversarial agency, carve the same activity into overlapping, instructive shapes.
